  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🥶 Drill Rapper Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Short, cold, punchy tags for UK and New York drill."
    },
    "intro": "Good drill names are short and cold: a nickname and an initial, a letter and a number, or a Lil, Big or Baby in front. This **drill rapper name generator** makes UK and New York drill tags, duo names and mixtape titles, kept clean for every listener.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good drill names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, for UK and NY drill:</p><ul><li><strong>Tezzy T5</strong>: a UK nickname with a letter and a number on the end.</li><li><strong>Zeph Ten</strong>: a short tag with a number spelled out.</li><li><strong>Kemzy V</strong>: the UK drill shape: a nickname ending in -zy and an initial.</li><li><strong>Rell Frost</strong>: a New York name, cold by design.</li><li><strong>Bobby Dinero</strong>: a NY name with a bit of swagger.</li><li><strong>Tremz x Tayo</strong>: a duo joined with an x, the way collabs are billed.</li><li><strong>Stairwell Diaries</strong>: a UK mixtape title about where it was written.</li><li><strong>Winter Training</strong>: a NY mixtape title, cold and hard-working.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How drill names work",
        "content": "<p>Drill began in Chicago in the early 2010s, with Chief Keef among its first stars. It took root in south London soon after, where groups like 67 and Harlem Spartans shaped UK drill. Then it crossed back to New York: Brooklyn drill grew in the late 2010s, and Pop Smoke worked with UK producers such as 808Melo.</p><p>The names differ a little on each side of the Atlantic:</p><ul><li><strong>UK drill</strong> tags are short nicknames, often ending in <em>-zy</em>, <em>-o</em> or <em>-z</em>, with a letter or number: <em>Kwamz C</em>, <em>Javo Seven</em>, <em>Y-Zero</em>.</li><li><strong>NY drill</strong> leans on Lil, Big and Baby, a first name and a flashy surname: <em>Big Teddy</em>, <em>Rocco Flossy</em>, <em>Baby Ricky</em>.</li><li><strong>Duo</strong> names are two tags joined with x or &amp;, and <strong>mixtapes</strong> are about the cold and the climb: <em>From the Cold</em>, <em>No Struggle, No Glory</em>.</li></ul><p>Every name here is kept clean, with nothing about violence or real people.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Make sure nobody else has it.</strong> Short tags are popular, so search YouTube, Spotify and Instagram before you upload anything.</li><li><strong>Keep it easy to say on a beat.</strong> One or two syllables and a letter works best in an ad-lib.</li></ul><p>For a rap name in any style, try the <a href=\"rapper-name-generator.html\">Rapper Name Generator</a>. For drill's Atlanta cousin, there's the <a href=\"trap-name-generator.html\">Trap Name Generator</a>, and for the tracks, the <a href=\"song-title-generator.html\">Song Title Generator</a>.</p><p>For more genres, see all the <a href=\"../categories/music_genres-names.html\">music genre name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good drill rapper names?",
        "answer": "Short, cold tags: a nickname with a letter or number (Tezzy T5, Kemzy V) for UK drill, or a Lil, Big or Baby name (Big Teddy, Baby Ricky) for New York drill."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's the difference between UK and NY drill names?",
        "answer": "UK drill names tend to be nicknames with an initial or number on the end. New York drill names use more Lil, Big and Baby, and flashy surnames like Dinero. Choose a style for names in each."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where did drill music come from?",
        "answer": "Chicago, in the early 2010s. It spread to south London, where UK drill developed its own sound, and from there to Brooklyn in the late 2010s."
      }
    ]
  }
